Ushell provides a graphical shell consisting of a main panel and a status bar
the main panel can be opened by pressing "alt+tab" ("mod1 mod2"), showing all available applications
to close main panel press "escape"
to go to previous application, press "alt tab" again
applications can be selected by typing their name, or using the arrow keys
	some characters in an application's name, are slightly highlighted
	entering them is enough to select that application
	to reset the selection, press "backspace"
press "space" or "enter" to open the selected application in its own workspace
any extra windows opened by the application, will be shown in a modal floating window
to close a window, press "alt escape" ("mod2 mod1")

right after opening the main panel, or after reseting selection using "backspace",
	press space to open the command prompt
by typing special keywords in command prompt, you have instant access to various small but useful things like:
	terminal, screenshot, calculator, unit conversion, timer, date at different timezones, weather, dictionary, etc
for example, by typing "t" followed by a "space", the terminal mode will be activated,
	and the entered command will be run in a terminal
the list of produced outputs will be placed under the prompt
	the user can navigate between them, using "ctrl page'up/page'down" 
for interactive commands, the focus will be moved to the output view
	to go back to the prompt, just press "ctrl page'up"

right after opening the main panel, or after reseting selection using "backspace", press "enter" to open system menu:
, session (lock, suspend, exit, reboot, poweroff)
, connections
, timezone
, autostart
, passwords
, packages (install, remove, update, create an installation media)
, backup (create, restore)

to have a really fail'safe system, redundant units are necessary
you can create a backup on a removable storage device
backups will be done using "BTRFS send", so they will be incremental and fast
the backup device is encrypted, and contains a backup of your home directory
	dencryption can be done using a password, as well as a key stored on the computer
each time the device is connected, the backup procedure will be run automatically
even in case of bit rot for data in home directory, this procedure repairs it automatically
this backup can easily be restored on a new system
these systems will then form an F2F network using GNUnet, through which they will be synced to each other

the ultimate goal is to use Ushell and Uni as the user interface of UPM Linux
but before it's ready, we can create a Chimera Linux based system, with Ushell and Uni as the user interface
to do that, boot into a Chimera Linux live image, and run:
; doas sh ./chimera.sh
